      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to find the greatest common divisor (GCD) and the least common multiple (LCM) of the numbers 18 and 42. Then we will find the product of these two values.\n\nFirst, we find the prime factorizations of 18 and 42:\n\\[\n18 = 2 \\times 3^2\n\\]\n\\[\n42 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 7\n\\]\n\nThe GCD is the product of the lowest power of all prime factors common to both numbers:\n\\[\n\\text{GCD}(18, 42) = 2^1 \\times 3^1 = 2 \\times 3 = 6\n\\]\n\nThe LCM is the product of the highest power of all prime factors present in the factorizations:\n\\[\n\\text{LCM}(18, 42) = 2^1 \\times 3^2 \\times 7^1 = 2 \\times 9 \\times 7 = 126\n\\]\n\nNow, we find the product of the GCD and the LCM:\n\\[\n\\text{Product} = \\text{GCD}(18, 42) \\times \\text{LCM}(18, 42) = 6 \\times 126 = 756\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of the GCD and the LCM of 18 and 42 is \\boxed{756}.",
