      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find the greatest common divisor (GCD) and the least common multiple (LCM) of the numbers 18 and 42, and then find their product.\n\nFirst, we find the prime factorizations of the numbers:\n\\[\n18 = 2 \\times 3^2\n\\]\n\\[\n42 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 7\n\\]\n\nThe GCD is the product of the lowest powers of all prime factors common to both numbers. The common prime factors of 18 and 42 are 2 and 3. The lowest power of 2 common to both is \\(2^1\\) and the lowest power of 3 common to both is \\(3^1\\). Therefore, the GCD is:\n\\[\n\\text{GCD}(18, 42) = 2 \\times 3 = 6\n\\]\n\nNext, we find the LCM. The LCM is the product of the highest powers of all prime factors involved in the factorizations. The prime factors involved are 2, 3, and 7. The highest power of 2 involved is \\(2^1\\), the highest power of 3 involved is \\(3^2\\), and the highest power of 7 involved is \\(7^1\\). Therefore, the LCM is:\n\\[\n\\text{LCM}(18, 42) = 2^1 \\times 3^2 \\times 7^1 = 2 \\times 9 \\times 7 = 126\n\\]\n\nNow, we find the product of the GCD and the LCM:\n\\[\n\\text{GCD}(18, 42) \\times \\text{LCM}(18, 42) = 6 \\times 126 = 756\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of the greatest common divisor and the least common multiple of 18 and 42 is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{756}\n\\]",
