      "text": "To find the product of the greatest common divisor (GCD) and the least common multiple (LCM) of \\(18\\) and \\(42\\), we first need to determine their GCD and LCM.\n\n**Step 1: Find the prime factorizations of the numbers.**\n\nFor \\(18\\):\n\\[\n18 = 2 \\times 3^2\n\\]\n\nFor \\(42\\):\n\\[\n42 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 7\n\\]\n\n**Step 2: Determine the GCD.**\n\nThe GCD is the product of the lowest power of each prime factor that appears in both factorizations. The common prime factors of \\(18\\) and \\(42\\) are \\(2\\) and \\(3\\):\n\\[\n\\text{GCD} = 2^1 \\times 3^1 = 2 \\times 3 = 6\n\\]\n\n**Step 3: Determine the LCM.**\n\nThe LCM is the product of the highest power of each prime factor that appears in the factorizations:\n\\[\n\\text{LCM} = 2^1 \\times 3^2 \\times 7^1 = 2 \\times 9 \\times 7 = 126\n\\]\n\n**Step 4: Calculate the product of the GCD and the LCM.**\n\n\\[\n\\text{Product} = \\text{GCD} \\times \\text{LCM} = 6 \\times 126\n\\]\n\n**Step 5: Perform the multiplication.**\n\n\\[\n6 \\times 126 = 6 \\times (120 + 6) = (6 \\times 120) + (6 \\times 6) = 720 + 36 = 756\n\\]\n\nHence, the product of the greatest common divisor and the least common multiple of \\(18\\) and \\(42\\) is \\(\\boxed{756}\\).",
