      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find the greatest common divisor (GCD) and the least common multiple (LCM) of the numbers 18 and 42, and then multiply these two values together.\n\nFirst, let's find the GCD of 18 and 42. The prime factorization of 18 is:\n\\[ 18 = 2 \\times 3^2 \\]\nThe prime factorization of 42 is:\n\\[ 42 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 7 \\]\nThe GCD is the product of the lowest power of each prime factor common to both numbers. The common prime factors are 2 and 3. The lowest power of 2 common to both is \\(2^1\\) and the lowest power of 3 common to both is \\(3^1\\). Therefore, the GCD is:\n\\[ \\text{GCD} = 2^1 \\times 3^1 = 2 \\times 3 = 6 \\]\n\nNext, let's find the LCM of 18 and 42. The LCM is the product of the highest power of each prime factor present in the factorizations of the numbers. From the prime factorizations we already have:\n\\[ 18 = 2 \\times 3^2 \\]\n\\[ 42 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 7 \\]\nThe LCM is:\n\\[ \\text{LCM} = 2^1 \\times 3^2 \\times 7^1 = 2 \\times 9 \\times 7 = 126 \\]\n\nNow, we need to find the product of the GCD and the LCM:\n\\[ \\text{GCD} \\times \\text{LCM} = 6 \\times 126 \\]\n\nTo calculate \\(6 \\times 126\\):\n\\[ 6 \\times 126 = 6 \\times (100 + 26) = 6 \\times 100 + 6 \\times 26 = 600 + 156 = 756 \\]\n\nTherefore, the product of the greatest common divisor and the least common multiple of 18 and 42 is:\n\\[ \\boxed{756} \\]",
